e list, and gold still tags it SpecificDisease, reinforcing that deficiency/absence-of-enzyme nominals get disease status in gold even when functioning as molecular descriptors, while the model treats them as O since no named disease head appears. The contrast cases are true positives like \"G6PD deficiency\" or named syndromes (\"Coats disease\", \"Norrie disease\", \"fragile X syndrome\") that carry clear disease nosology.\n\nI'm noting the trigger boils down to whether the head noun is a generic deficit term (deficiency/absence/deficient activity) taking an enzyme or protein complement via \"of/in\", versus a compound where the disease-type noun itself is the head. \"Fever\" appears as an outlier true positive that's really just a symptom, likely annotation noise rather than a systematic pattern.\n\nI'm also tracing a position discrepancy for the G6PD deficiency span — the gold annotation at one location doesn't match the \"deficiency in G6PD\" snippet, since the actual gold span for \"G6PD deficiency\" appears earlier in the text in a different sentence about prevalence in African populations, so I need to verify which occurrence the gold label actually corresponds to.\n\nSince the gold offsets [772,787] don't literally match \"deficiency in G6PD\" in the text, there's likely an alignment issue—maybe the span is actually \"G6PD deficiency\" elsewhere, and this same doc already has that phrase annotated as a true positive SpecificDisease elsewhere. I shouldn't dwell on this too much and should move forward with the analysis.\n\nSame pattern appears with \"absence of the seventh component of complement\" and other PP constructions — the generalizable feature seems to be whether a loss-of-function noun (deficiency/absence/deficient activity) is followed by an \"of/in\" prepositional phrase versus preceded by a gene symbol as a compound modifier, since the former reads as describing a protein property rather than a disease condition. I notice three of the four discr